.Dd April 2, 2026
.Dt EX 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm ex
.Nd line-oriented text editor with visual handoff support
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl RrsSv
.Op Fl c Ar command
.Op Fl t Ar tag
.Op Ar +command
.Op Ar file
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is Substrate's shared-core line editor.
It provides a BSD-first
line-editor command language backed by the shared
.Pa usr.lib/exvi
implementation used by both
.Nm
and
.Xr vi 1 .
.Pp
Commands are read from standard input in batch mode or from the controlling
terminal in interactive mode.
Interactive sessions display a
.Ql \&:
prompt before each command.
.Pp
The current supported command contract includes:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It visual handoff
.Ic visual ,
.Ic vi
.It version
.Ic version ,
.Ic ver
.It argument list
.Ic args ,
.Ic ar ,
.Ic next ,
.Ic n ,
.Ic prev ,
.Ic rewind ,
.Ic rew
.It recovery and session control
.Ic preserve ,
.Ic pre ,
.Ic recover ,
.Ic rec ,
.Ic pop ,
.Ic po
.It tags and options
.Ic tag ,
.Ic ta ,
.Ic tags ,
.Ic set ,
.Ic se
.It file lifecycle
.Ic quit ,
.Ic q ,
.Ic xit ,
.Ic x ,
.Ic wq ,
.Ic write ,
.Ic w ,
.Ic edit ,
.Ic e ,
.Ic read ,
.Ic r
.It line and buffer operations
.Ic delete ,
.Ic d ,
.Ic undo ,
.Ic u ,
.Ic put ,
.Ic pu ,
.Ic print ,
.Ic p ,
.Ic number ,
.Ic \&# ,
.Ic list ,
.Ic l ,
.Ic = ,
.Ic mark ,
.Ic ma ,
.Ic k Ns Ar letter ,
.Ic file ,
.Ic f ,
.Ic append ,
.Ic a ,
.Ic insert ,
.Ic i ,
.Ic change ,
.Ic c
.It copy and movement operations
.Ic copy ,
.Ic co ,
.Ic t ,
.Ic move ,
.Ic m ,
.Ic join ,
.Ic j ,
.Ic yank ,
.Ic y
.It regex and shell operations
.Ic substitute ,
.Ic s ,
.Ic & ,
.Ic global ,
.Ic g ,
.Ic v ,
.Ic \&!
.El
.Pp
Addresses, ranges, marks, search addresses, relative offsets, and
delimiter-aware command chaining with
.Ql |
are supported.
The editor accepts the traditional
.Ql \&"
command-tail comment form outside delimiter bodies.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl R
Start in readonly mode.
Plain writes are rejected unless forced with
.Ql \&! .
.It Fl r
Recover the named file from a preserved recovery image.
If no recovery image exists,
.Nm
fails with an error.
.It Fl s
Batch mode: suppress interactive diagnostics where possible, and read no
startup commands from
.Ev EXINIT ,
.Pa ~/.exrc
or
.Pa ./.exrc .
Commands given with
.Fl c
still run.
.It Fl S
Start in secure mode.
Shell escapes and shell-capable commands are disabled.
.It Fl v
Enter visual mode by handing control to
.Xr vi 1 .
.It Fl c Ar command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
This option may be specified more than once.
.It Fl t Ar tag
Start by editing the file and location named by
.Ar tag .
.It Ar +command
Execute
.Ar command
after startup processing.
Common forms include
.Ql +42
to move to line 42 and
.Ql +next
to advance within the argument list.
.It Ar file
Open
.Ar file
as the current buffer.
If multiple files are given, they become the initial argument list.
.El
.Sh SET OPTIONS
The shared
.Ic :set
option table currently supports:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ic number , nu
Number printed output in
.Nm .
.It Ic list , li
Render tabs and line endings in list form.
.It Ic autoindent , ai
Shared option state used by the visual frontend's indentation helpers.
.It Ic ignorecase , ic
Make searches,
.Ic global ,
.Ic v ,
and
.Ic substitute
case-insensitive.
.It Ic readonly , ro
Mark the session readonly.
.It Ic showmode , smd
Shared option state used by the visual frontend's mode banner.
.It Ic wrapscan , ws
Wrap searches at buffer boundaries.
.It Ic writeany , wa
Let
.Ic write
replace an existing file other than the current one without
.Ql \&! .
It is off by default.
.It Ic exrc , ex
Read
.Pa ./.exrc
at startup.
It is off by default and has effect only when set by
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc .
.It Ic tabstop= , ts=
Set tab rendering width.
The default is 8.
.It Ic scroll= , sc=
Set the visual frontend's half-page scroll amount once explicitly configured.
.It Ic tags=
Set the comma-separated tag-file search path used by
.Ic tag
and related visual commands.
.It Ic directory= , dir=
The directory under which the user's recovery directory,
.Pa exvi- Ns Ar uid ,
is kept.
The default is
.Pa /var/tmp .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ev EXINIT
If set, supplies startup commands, and
.Pa ~/.exrc
is not loaded.
Ignored with
.Fl s .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.exrc
Per-user startup commands, loaded when
.Ev EXINIT
is not set, and only when ownership and directory safety checks pass.
.It Pa ./.exrc
Per-directory startup commands, loaded after
.Ev EXINIT
or
.Pa ~/.exrc
only if one of them set the
.Ic exrc
option, only when the ownership and directory safety checks pass, and not
when it is
.Pa ~/.exrc
itself.
Neither
.Pa .exrc
is loaded with
.Fl s
or
.Fl S .
.It Pa tags
Default tag file name when the
.Ic tags
option is left at its default value.
Each line is a tag name, a file name and an address, separated by tabs.
The address is a line number or a
.Li / Ns Ar pattern Ns Li /
or
.Li \&? Ns Ar pattern Ns Li \&?
search, and nothing else: any other address is reported as a bad tag
address, never run as a command.
.It Pa /var/tmp/exvi- Ns Ar uid
The user's recovery directory, under the
.Ic directory
option's value: preserved recovery images, one per file, written by
.Ic preserve
and for sessions ended by
.Dv SIGHUP
or
.Dv SIGTERM .
It is created with mode 0700 and used only while it is the user's, not a
symbolic link and closed to everyone else; each image is created afresh
with mode 0600, and only an image the user owns is recovered.
Writing the file removes an image only if this session wrote or recovered
from it.
.El
.Sh ASYNCHRONOUS EVENTS
.Dv SIGINT
stops the current command (a
.Ic global
between the commands it runs) and ends text input;
.Nm
writes
.Ql Interrupt
and returns to the command prompt with the buffer as the command left it.
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Dv SIGTERM
preserve a modified buffer and end the session.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 on success and with a value greater than 0 if an error occurs.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ex file.txt
ex +42 file.txt
ex -c 'set number' -c '%p' file.txt
ex -R file.txt
ex -r file.txt
ex -t main tags-demo.c
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The supported Substrate contract is intentionally narrower than the union of
historical BSD,
nvi,
and Vim command sets.
Only the command names, abbreviations, and
.Ic :set
spellings documented here and in
.Pa docs/specs/exvi.md
are part of the supported interface.
.Pp
Readonly entry points such as
view
and restricted entry points such as
.Ql rex
are implemented through the shared frontend parser even though they are not
separate binaries today.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vi 1
